+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    The versions to sort. All of them must be valid semver versions.
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text,
+one version per line, which makes the output easy to consume in a
+shell loop::
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 | while read v; do echo "found $v"; done
+    found 1.2.0
+    found 1.10.0
+
+If no versions are given as arguments, they are read from *standard in*,
+one per line (blank lines are ignored)::
+
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+If there are no versions at all, nothing is printed. If any version is
+invalid, an error message is printed on standard error and nothing
+is printed on standard out.
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Print the highest version that is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    The versions to search. All of them must be valid semver versions.
+
+Prereleases are skipped, build metadata is ignored when comparing::
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.2.0 1.10.0-rc.1 1.9.0
+    1.9.0
+
+If no versions are given as arguments, they are read from *standard in*,
+one per line (blank lines are ignored)::
+
+    $ printf '1.2.0\n1.10.0\n2.0.0-rc.1\n' | pysemver latest
+    1.10.0
+
+If there are no versions, or all of them are prereleases, nothing is
+printed. If any version is invalid, an error message is printed on
+standard error and nothing is printed on standard out.
+
+
 Return Code
 -----------
 
